From 9dd03d52559a25cd4a6e22fcb3940f87fce390d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cedric Robert <cedric.robert@f2i-consulting.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:54:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Sized reads: the AbstractObject read helpers take a Span,
 handed to the proxy for the external parts

The representations read their arrays through the AbstractObject helpers
(gsoap array -> buffer): readArrayNdOfDoubleValues, readArrayNdOfFloatValues,
readArrayNdOfIntegerValues<T>, readArrayNdOfBooleanValues, for RESQML 2.0.1
and EML 2.3. Each gets a form taking a COMMON_NS::Span<T> instead of a bare
pointer; the pointer forms are untouched.

- For an external array (HDF5 dataset in 2.0.1, external data array parts
  in 2.3), the sized proxy read of patch 1 is called: the file proxy checks
  the dataset extent, the ETP proxy sizes its request. In 2.3 each part gets
  the slice of the buffer its Count announces, and a part that would overrun
  the buffer is refused with a std::range_error before any read.
- For a constant, lattice, range or XML array, the values and their count
  are in the XML: the pointer form does the work.
- The sixteen integer specializations are one-liners over one private
  template per standard version, readIntegerValuesSized<T>, built on the
  readArrayNd<T> dispatch of AbstractHdfProxy.

A representation migrates by computing the count it already uses to size
its buffer and passing Span<T>(buffer, count) to the helper; the next
patches do it file by file.
